## Statistical limitations, found by this repo's own validation

- **SRM has limited power against subtle, selective loss.** A {{ "%.0f" | format(100 * s2.E_srm.selective_srm_example.spec.srm_drop) }}% selective loss of treated users at n = {{ "{:,}".format(s2.E_srm.selective_srm_example.spec.n) }} was flagged in only {{ s2.E_srm.selective_srm_example.srm_flag_rate | pct(0) }} of simulations, and a pre-period balance check (|SMD| > 0.05) in only {{ s2.E_srm.selective_srm_example["pre_metric_smd_gt_0.05_flag_rate"] | pct(0) }}, while the estimate was biased (coverage {{ s2.E_srm.selective_srm_example.naive_estimator.coverage | pct(0) }}). A random 1% loss at n = 100k was flagged {{ s2.E_srm.detection_rate_by_drop["0.01"] | pct(1) }} of the time.
- **IPW under poor overlap** undercovers ({{ o.O6_poor_overlap.results.ipw.coverage | pct(1) }}) because its SE understates the variability (SE/SD = {{ "%.2f" | format(o.O6_poor_overlap.results.ipw.se_ratio) }}). Use AIPW, or trim and state the changed estimand.
- **IPW with bootstrap SE** covered {{ o.O2_ipw_bootstrap.results.ipw_bootstrap.coverage | pct(1) }} over {{ o.O2_ipw_bootstrap.results.ipw_bootstrap.n_reps }} replications (100 bootstrap draws each). That is slightly under nominal and within Monte Carlo noise of 95%. More draws would be needed to say more.
- **IPW/AIPW on a binary outcome** {% set b3 = o.O3_binary_measured_confounding.results %}covered {{ b3.ipw.coverage | pct(1) }} / {{ b3.aipw.coverage | pct(1) }} over {{ b3.ipw.n_reps }} replications (MC SE ≈ {{ '%.1f' | format(100 * (0.95 * 0.05 / b3.ipw.n_reps) ** 0.5) }} pp): {{ '%.1f' | format(100 * (0.95 - b3.ipw.coverage)) }}-{{ '%.1f' | format(100 * (0.95 - b3.aipw.coverage)) }} pp under nominal.
- **The pre-trend test is weak.** A 0.03/period violation biased DiD by {{ "%.3f" | format(p.D2_small_trend_violation.results.twfe_cluster.bias) }} and dropped coverage to {{ p.D2_small_trend_violation.results.twfe_cluster.coverage | pct(0) }}, but the test flagged it only {{ p.D2_small_trend_violation.pretrend_test_rejection_rate | pct(1) }} of the time. Honest-DiD style sensitivity bounds (Rambachan & Roth) would be the next step.
- **Uplift on rare binary outcomes needs a lot of data.** At 10k training rows the learners rarely produced a Qini CI excluding zero ({{ u.U2_binary_step_hte.summary.x_learner.qini_ci_excludes_zero_rate | pct(0) }}) even with a real 0-vs-4pp split.
- **Newcombe CIs are conservative at very small counts** ({{ s2.A_effects.binary_small_counts.results.newcombe.coverage | pct(1) }} coverage at n = 600, p = 1%). Wald was close to nominal there ({{ s2.A_effects.binary_small_counts.results.wald.coverage | pct(1) }}) but undefined in {{ s2.A_effects.binary_small_counts.results.wald.n_failed }} of {{ s2.A_effects.binary_small_counts.results.wald.n_reps }} samples.

## Scope limitations

- **DiD supports block adoption only** (one treatment date). Staggered adoption with heterogeneous effects biases two-way fixed effects (Goodman-Bacon 2021). Callaway-Sant'Anna or Sun-Abraham estimators are future work.
- **DiD has no real-data case study.** I found no suitable public panel with a genuine policy change and did not invent one.
- **The sequential design covers efficacy stopping only.** Futility bounds, unequal-information re-computation and always-valid (mSPRT) inference are not implemented.
- **CUPED uses a single covariate**, with theta estimated on the pooled sample. Lin regression handles several covariates. CUPAC (ML-predicted covariates) is not implemented.
- **Ratio metrics** (e.g. revenue per session with user randomization) need the delta method on the ratio. Only clustered means and relative lift are provided.
- **Uplift features in Criteo are anonymised**, so the targeting rule can't be explained or audited for fairness. The Criteo licence (CC BY-NC-SA 4.0) restricts use to non-commercial purposes.

## Case-study limitations

- **GA360 is observational, and purchase intent is unmeasured.** Cross-device journeys are invisible because cookies are per device. The suspected-internal segment ({{ ga.internal_traffic.share_of_desktop_conversions | pct(0) }} of desktop conversions) is identified by pattern, not confirmed. GA360 timestamps are UTC, so hour-of-day is not local time.
- **Hillstrom's profit decision** depends entirely on assumed margin and e-mail cost (`configs/hillstrom.yaml`).
- **Criteo uses a {{ (crit.n_rows / 13979592) | pct(1) }} seeded sample** for speed. The economic assumptions are illustrative, and the memo reports how the decision changes across them.

## Engineering notes

- On macOS with Apple Accelerate and NumPy 2.2 (the Python 3.10 lock), scikit-learn's logistic fit emits spurious `RuntimeWarning: overflow encountered in matmul`. All results are finite and the tests pass. The warnings are not suppressed.
- Full validation (`make validate`) takes roughly 15 minutes on a laptop. CI runs the quick versions only.
